<article class="env-lemma" id="0FSN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FSN">Lemma <span data-tag="0FSN">24.25.1</span>.</a> Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site. Let $\mathcal{A}$ be a sheaf of graded algebras on $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$. There exists a set $T$ and for each $t \in T$ an injective map $\mathcal{N}_ t \to \mathcal{N}'_ t$ of graded $\mathcal{A}$-modules such that an object $\mathcal{I}$ of $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{A})$ is injective if and only if for every solid diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ \mathcal{N}_ t \ar[r] \ar[d] &  \mathcal{I} \\  \mathcal{N}'_ t \ar@{..>}[ru] }  \]
</div>
  <p> a dotted arrow exists in $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{A})$ making the diagram commute. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true in any Grothendieck abelian category, see Injectives, Lemma <a href="/tag/079G" data-tag="079G">19.11.6</a>. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0FRD" data-tag="0FRD">24.11.1</a> the category $\textit{Mod}(\mathcal{A})$ is a Grothendieck abelian category. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>